TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "action": "final",
  "output": {
    "research_concepts": [
      "role-based lineup composition",
      "lineup coverage",
      "organizer",
      "shaper",
      "terminal",
      "within-team variation",
      "talent control",
      "structural roles"
    ],
    "methodological_questions": [
      "role assignment reliability",
      "confounding by injury/rest",
      "small lineup sample sizes",
      "definition of talent control",
      "separability from talent metrics",
      "within-team variance decomposition"
    ],
    "historical_evidence": [],
    "failed_approaches": [],
    "evidence_establishes": [
      "No prior work was retrieved for this exact question; thus nothing is established or falsified from historical records."
    ],
    "evidence_does_not_establish": [
      "Historical evidence is absent, so it does not establish testability, effect sizes, or potential pitfalls for this specific question."
    ],
    "own_inferences": [
      "The question is testable in principle with the available data, assuming reliable role definitions are available from independent sources or prior seasons.",
      "Lineup coverage (presence of at least one organizer, shaper, terminal) is a possession-level binary that can be computed from play-by-play and substitution data.",
      "Within-team variation in coverage is likely to exist mechanically unless all of a team's lineups include all three roles; the nontrivial aspect is whether this variation persists after controlling for player availability and talent.",
      "Separability from talent/counts can be examined by regressing coverage on team-level talent metrics and testing for remaining variance.",
      "Talent control behaviour likely refers to checking that the coverage–outcome relationship remains stable when talent is included as a covariate, a test of robust inference.",
      "Even with 420k possessions, statistical power for interaction or within-team variance tests may be limited if coverage is rare."
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The exact method for defining organizer, shaper, terminal roles is not specified; if derived from the same play-type data used for analysis, circularity and lookahead bias could invalidate tests.",
      "It is unclear whether roles are player-level (stable across the season) or possession-level (which would require real-time identification and risk measurement error).",
      "The outcome variable that talent control should stabilise is unstated; the question may be incomplete without a specific outcome of interest.",
      "Confounding from coaching decisions, game state, and injuries may be severe, and traditional controls may not fully address endogeneity.",
      "Within-team variance in coverage may be so small in some teams that it is indistinguishable from noise, reducing power."
    ],
    "historical_effect": {
      "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
      "explanation": "No prior work was retrieved, so the decision is based entirely on the current methodological assessment of the question and data."
    },
    "testability": {
      "p_testable": 0.75,
      "blocking_constraints": [
        {
          "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
          "text": "The reliability and validity of role assignments (organizer, shaper, terminal) are critical. If roles are not defined a priori with high inter-rater or out-of-sample consistency, the exposure variable is noisy and tests will fail."
        },
        {
          "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
          "text": "If most teams have coverage in >95% or <5% of possessions, within-team variation is virtually absent, making variance-based tests uninformative."
        },
        {
          "code": "CONFOUNDING",
          "text": "Lineup selection is endogenous (coach decisions, injuries, matchups) and may create spurious correlations between coverage and outcomes that are not separable from talent."
        },
        {
          "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
          "text": "If role definitions are built from the same game events that are used to measure coverage and outcomes, temporal leakage artificially inflates associations."
        }
      ]
    },
    "prior_work": {
      "already_answered": "unknown",
      "cited": [],
      "summary": "No earlier studies from this project were found that directly address lineup coverage defined by organizer, shaper, terminal roles or its separability from talent."
    },
    "risks": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Roles must be assigned reliably; ambiguous or noisy role labels will attenuate or create spurious coverage effects."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "If the proportion of possessions with coverage is very high or very low for a given team, within-team variation is minimal and difficult to detect."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Coverage variation is not randomised; game context, opponent strength, score, fatigue, and injury influence lineups and may be correlated with talent metrics."
      },
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "Defining roles using the same game data that enters the analysis creates a lookahead bias unless a strict train/test split is used."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Although total possessions are large, the effective sample for within-team variance or interaction tests may be limited by clustering and sparse coverage states."
      },
      {
        "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
        "text": "If a team lacks a player of a given role entirely, coverage is impossible for all of its lineups, introducing a mechanical floor; constraining variance."
      },
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "Misclassification of players into roles will bias estimates of coverage variation and its relationship with talent."
      },
      {
        "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
        "text": "Testing three related hypotheses (within-team variation, separability, talent control) increases false positive risk unless corrected."
      },
      {
        "code": "IMPLEMENTATION_BUG",
        "text": "Complex merging of play-by-play, substitutions, and role data may produce errors in coverage assignment."
      },
      {
        "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
        "text": "Player roles may evolve over time; using a static role assignment from prior seasons can introduce measurement drift, especially for younger players or those changing teams."
      }
    ],
    "predicted_outcome": {
      "supported": 0.2,
      "rejected": 0.1,
      "untestable": 0.2,
      "artifact": 0.1,
      "uncertain": 0.4
    },
    "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
    "design": {
      "estimand": "The within-team variance of possession-level lineup coverage (binary: 1 if lineup contains ≥1 organizer, shaper, terminal; 0 otherwise) across stints, and the residual variation after controlling for team-level talent (average player talent score) and player-count availability.",
      "unit": "Possession (offensive or defensive stance). Primary analysis aggregates to stint-level (uninterrupted lineup stretch) to reduce autocorrelation.",
      "sample": "NBA 2025-26 regular season and playoffs; approximately 210,000 possessions, 30 team-seasons. Roles built from play-type data of 2023-24 and 2024-25 seasons (training set) to avoid leakage.",
      "power": "Postulated coverage rate of 0.6 with a standard deviation across stints of 0.15. For a within-team likelihood ratio test of variance components (team random effect), 30 teams and 70 stints per team yield >80% power to detect a coverage ICC of 0.1 (moderate within-team clustering). Separability test (difference in R²) would require at most a medium effect size (f²=0.15) with 30 teams, which is borderline; we plan a sensitivity analysis with resampling.",
      "controls": "Team-level talent: holistic metric summing minutes-weighted player offensive/defensive role scores (organizer, shaper, terminal scores) averaged over the training set. Per-possession controls: game quarter, score margin (to nearest 5 points), home/away, opponent defensive rating, indicator for back-to-back, and whether starter is missing (injury/rest proxy). Player-count availability: number of available players of each role on the roster for that game.",
      "falsification": "Placebo test: randomise role labels among players of the same position; observed within-team variance should collapse. Negative control: coverage defined with a sham role (e.g., 'rim protector' based on blocks) should not show the same separability pattern. If coverage is purely a talent proxy, including an interaction between talent and stint-level usage of role players should absorb the coverage variance.",
      "multiplicity": "Three primary tests: (1) likelihood ratio test for within-team variance component; (2) partial F-test for coverage after regressing out talent and count controls; (3) stability constancy test comparing coverage coefficient across nested talent models via bootstrapped 95% confidence interval overlap. We will use Holm-Bonferroni correction across the three tests.",
      "decision_rule": "Confirm supporting evidence if (1) within-team variance significantly > 0 at α/3; (2) coverage adds significant explanatory power (Δ R² > 0.02) over talent-only model at α/3; (3) the coverage coefficient changes by ≤ 20% and sign remains stable when talent controls are added, with bootstrapped equivalence test at α/3.",
      "data_version": "NBA data as of 2026-07-13; play-by-play and substitution data from 2022-23 through 2025-26; roles trained on 2023-24 and 2024-25 regular seasons. Exclude possessions with fewer than 5 players on the floor or garbage time (margin >20 in 4th quarter)."
    }
  }
}

